<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dos Pilas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded 629</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dos Pilas is a Maya site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petexbatun">Petexbatún</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, founded by an offshoot of the Tikal dynasty in 629 CE to control trade along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasion-river">Pasión River</a>. In 648 its ruler B'alaj Chan K'awiil, who claimed the Tikal throne, broke away and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a>; Tikal captured the city in 672, but Dos Pilas defeated Tikal in 679, conquered neighbouring cities and ruled with Aguateca as twin capitals of the Petexbatún kingdom. After Calakmul's defeat, Tamarindito and other centres rebelled, and in 761 the city was abandoned. Refugees threw up walls of stone stripped from its temples and palaces, but their village was overrun early in the 9th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dos-pilas.jpg" alt="Dos Pilas" width="330" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joanbanjo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panell_19,_Dos_Pilas,_museu_Nacional_d'Arqueologia_i_Etnologia,_Guatemala_(MUNAE).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Worse came when <b>Dos Pilas</b>, an outpost ruled by a Tikal prince, B'alaj Chan K'awiil, went over to Kaanul: in 657 Calakmul's king Yuknoom Ch'een II drove Nuun Ujol Chaak from Tikal, and in 679 Dos Pilas defeated Tikal in battle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tikal">Tikal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The two greatest powers, Tikal and the Snake kingdom of Kaanul, ruled from Dzibanche and later from Calakmul, contended for supremacy through much of the 6th to 8th centuries, drawing in allies from Caracol to <b>Dos Pilas</b> and Naranjo.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The most powerful took the title kaloomte' and ruled other kings as vassals: in the 7th century Yuknoom Ch’een II of Calakmul controlled the kings of <b>Dos Pilas</b>, Cancuén and Naranjo.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classic-maya">Social Hierarchy of the Classic Maya</a></li></ul></section>
